---
import DocsCodeBlock from "../../components/docs/DocsCodeBlock.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocsRelated from "../../components/docs/DocsRelated.astro";

/* A move is `sandboxes move` in one sandbox, the machine agents on two computers, and two `ic` runs
   (_sandbox/ic/src/sandbox/device_move/), so the steps and rules below are theirs. */

const move = `sandboxes move translations --to z440 --why "z440 has the disk and stays on at night"`;

const compose = `sandboxes move translations --to z440 --manager compose`;

const back = `# On the computer it left, within 7 days: start the copy kept there.
ic sandbox start translations`;
---

<DocsLayout
    pageId="move-sandbox"
    heading="Move a sandbox to another computer"
    lead="Move one sandbox, with its files, its conversations, its credentials and its keys, from one of your computers to another. You approve one card. Nobody opens SSH, copies a volume or writes a Compose file by hand."
>
    <h2 id="before">What you need</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Both computers run the machine agent and are connected to the sandbox you run the move from.</li>
        <li>On both, the <strong>Manage sandboxes on this device</strong> switch is on, and the agent is current enough to offer moves.</li>
        <li>
            You run the move from a <em>different</em> sandbox than the one you move. A sandbox cannot move itself: it would cut off the
            conversation running the move halfway through.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="run">Run it</h2>
    <p>Ask an agent in any of your sandboxes, or run it in its terminal:</p>
    <DocsCodeBlock code={move} lang="bash" title="In another sandbox" />
    <p>
        You can name the sandbox by the name the app shows or by its slug. First it looks at the sandbox where it runs now and works out
        how much data will be copied. Then a card in your chat names the sandbox, the computer it leaves, the computer it goes to and the
        size, and says that the sandbox is offline while it is copied. Nothing happens until you approve.
    </p>
    <p>After you approve, the move runs in this order:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>The new computer gets ready while the sandbox keeps running.</strong> It checks Docker and free disk space (the data plus
            2 GB), downloads the same sandbox image, and builds your environment from the same recipe.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The sandbox stops and is copied.</strong> Its workspace, its history and its Docker data go across in one encrypted stream,
            with its credentials and its at-rest key. Each volume is checked file by file when it arrives.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>It starts on the new computer</strong>, set up as that computer's own sandbox. The move counts as done only when its tunnel
            is up again, so its address keeps working.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The old computer keeps its copy</strong>, stopped, for 7 days. After that it is removed on its own.
        </li>
    </ol>
    <p>The command prints each step and how many bytes have been copied, and ends with where the sandbox runs now.</p>

    <h2 id="compose">Run it with Docker Compose</h2>
    <p>
        By default the new computer runs the sandbox the way <code>ic</code> runs every sandbox. On a server you manage with Docker Compose or
        Komodo, ask for a Compose folder:
    </p>
    <DocsCodeBlock code={compose} lang="bash" title="Compose on the new computer" />
    <p>
        The sandbox then runs from <code>~/.intentic/compose/&lt;slug&gt;/</code> on that computer (<code>compose.yaml</code>, its{" "}
        <code>.env</code> and a copy of its at-rest key). For a Komodo stack, point the stack at that folder.
    </p>

    <h2 id="safety">What keeps it safe</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Nobody handles keys.</strong> Each computer makes a one-time key for this move. The sandbox running the move passes only the
            public halves between them, and no agent ever sees them.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The copy is encrypted end to end.</strong> The two computers connect directly on your network when they can. If they
            cannot, the sandbox running the move relays the stream between them, and all it sees is encrypted bytes. A stream that is changed,
            cut short or reordered is refused.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A key is never overwritten.</strong> If the new computer already has a different at-rest key for this sandbox, the move
            stops before anything is stopped.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Volumes are never overwritten.</strong> A volume with the same name and data already on the new computer stops the move,
            unless it is the copy an earlier move from that computer left behind.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="fails">If something fails</h2>
    <p>
        Before the new copy is up, any failure undoes both sides. The new computer removes what it created, and the old one starts the
        sandbox again as it was. Each computer does this on its own, so it still works if the sandbox running the move goes away. A computer
        that restarts in the middle of a move cleans up the next time <code>ic</code> runs there.
    </p>
    <p>
        In one case the old computer cannot tell what happened: everything was sent, and then the connection broke before the new computer
        said whether the sandbox started. The old copy then stays stopped, so two copies never run at once, and the command says so. Check
        the new computer. If the sandbox is not running there, start the old copy:
    </p>
    <DocsCodeBlock code={back} lang="bash" title="Bring it back" />

    <DocsRelated
        links={[
            { href: "/docs/your-machine/", title: "Your own machine", why: "the machine agent both computers run, and its switches." },
            { href: "/docs/docker/", title: "Docker setup", why: "the containers and volumes a sandbox is made of, which a move copies." },
            { href: "/docs/troubleshooting/", title: "Troubleshooting", why: "when a sandbox does not come up after a move." },
        ]}
    />
</DocsLayout>
